1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,896
Episode Seventy-One introduced Spring as a platform around an IoC container.

2
00:00:04,896 --> 00:00:09,856
Inversion of Control flips who constructs collaborators — the framework does.

3
00:00:09,856 --> 00:00:14,366
Dependency Injection is the main technique Spring uses to achieve IoC.

4
00:00:14,366 --> 00:00:19,391
Get this wrong — mysterious nulls, circular dependencies, and untestable code.

5
00:00:19,391 --> 00:00:23,707
Get this right — clear graphs, easy mocks, and predictable startup.

6
00:00:23,707 --> 00:00:28,474
Today — IoC versus DI, injection styles, bean scopes, and wiring pitfalls.

7
00:00:28,474 --> 00:00:30,378
Episode Seventy-Two.

8
00:00:30,378 --> 00:00:33,140
IoC and Dependency Injection.

9
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:35,837
IoC is the principle — DI is the mechanism.

10
00:00:35,837 --> 00:00:40,352
Inversion of Control — your code does not new up the whole object graph.

11
00:00:40,352 --> 00:00:44,868
Dependency Injection — dependencies are provided from outside the class.

12
00:00:44,868 --> 00:00:49,007
Spring's ApplicationContext is the injector and lifecycle manager.

13
00:00:49,007 --> 00:00:54,087
Factories, service locators, and events are other IoC styles — DI is the default.

14
00:00:54,087 --> 00:00:58,038
Say IoC for the idea — DI for how Spring usually implements it.

15
00:00:58,038 --> 00:01:01,123
Three injection styles you will see in Spring code.

16
00:01:01,123 --> 00:01:05,600
Constructor injection — required dependencies as final fields — preferred.

17
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:10,017
Setter injection — optional dependencies or reconfiguration after create.

18
00:01:10,017 --> 00:01:14,615
Field injection with Autowired — short, but harder to test and reason about.

19
00:01:14,615 --> 00:01:19,334
Constructor injection makes invariants obvious — object is complete after new.

20
00:01:19,334 --> 00:01:23,992
Modern Spring and Boot samples default to constructor injection for a reason.

21
00:01:23,992 --> 00:01:27,903
Bean scopes control how many instances the container creates.

22
00:01:27,903 --> 00:01:32,069
Singleton — one shared instance per context — the Spring default.

23
00:01:32,069 --> 00:01:35,916
Prototype — a new instance every time you ask the container.

24
00:01:35,916 --> 00:01:39,955
Request and session scopes — web-aware, tied to HTTP lifecycle.

25
00:01:39,955 --> 00:01:44,314
Wrong scope — shared mutable state across requests is a classic bug.

26
00:01:44,314 --> 00:01:49,250
Default to singleton services with immutable or carefully synchronized state.

27
00:01:49,250 --> 00:01:51,428
How Spring finds and wires beans.

28
00:01:51,428 --> 00:01:57,037
Component scanning picks up Stereotype annotations — Service, Repository, Controller.

29
00:01:57,037 --> 00:02:01,129
Configuration classes with Bean methods define explicit beans.

30
00:02:01,129 --> 00:02:05,221
Qualifiers disambiguate when multiple candidates share a type.

31
00:02:05,221 --> 00:02:09,708
Profiles activate environment-specific beans — local, staging, prod.

32
00:02:09,708 --> 00:02:14,724
Circular dependencies signal a design smell — break the cycle with redesign.

33
00:02:14,724 --> 00:02:16,649
DI pays off hardest in tests.

34
00:02:16,649 --> 00:02:21,029
Unit tests construct the class with mocks — no container required.

35
00:02:21,029 --> 00:02:25,276
Slice tests load a thin Spring context — web or data layer only.

36
00:02:25,276 --> 00:02:30,187
Full ApplicationContext tests catch wiring mistakes — slower but valuable.

37
00:02:30,187 --> 00:02:34,634
Avoid static singletons and ServiceLocator lookups — they fight DI.

38
00:02:34,634 --> 00:02:38,350
If you cannot inject a fake, the design is fighting you.

39
00:02:38,350 --> 00:02:39,771
Three common mistakes.

40
00:02:39,771 --> 00:02:44,488
One — field injection everywhere — invisible dependencies, awkward tests.

41
00:02:44,488 --> 00:02:48,430
Two — singleton beans holding request-specific mutable state.

42
00:02:48,430 --> 00:02:53,146
Three — Autowired on concrete classes with no interface — tight coupling.

43
00:02:53,146 --> 00:02:58,768
Also — ignoring constructor failure — missing beans explode at startup for good reason.

44
00:02:58,768 --> 00:03:03,872
Fail fast at context refresh — never with a NullPointerException in production.

45
00:03:03,872 --> 00:03:07,129
Interview question — why prefer constructor injection?

46
00:03:07,129 --> 00:03:10,265
Required dependencies are explicit and can be final.

47
00:03:10,265 --> 00:03:14,667
The object is fully initialized after construction — no half-ready beans.

48
00:03:14,667 --> 00:03:18,105
Unit tests pass mocks without Spring or reflection hacks.

49
00:03:18,105 --> 00:03:22,387
Circular dependencies surface earlier — design problems become visible.

50
00:03:22,387 --> 00:03:26,850
Spring and the community treat constructor injection as the default style.

51
00:03:26,850 --> 00:03:30,601
Wiring is clear — next is how projects start fast.

52
00:03:30,601 --> 00:03:33,827
Episode Seventy-Three — Spring Boot Basics.

53
00:03:33,827 --> 00:03:39,754
Starters, auto-configuration, configuration properties, and the executable jar.

54
00:03:39,754 --> 00:03:40,804
See you there.
